(; constants used by Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Basic, declared with their translated types ;)

(; doc: Commutative version of `AddCancelMonoid`. ;)
(; //LEAN:AddCancelCommMonoid ;)
AddCancelCommMonoid : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: An additive commutative group is an additive group with commutative `(+)`.  [Wikidata Q181296](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q181296) ;)
(; //LEAN:AddCommGroup ;)
AddCommGroup : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: An additive commutative monoid is an additive monoid with commutative `(+)`. ;)
(; //LEAN:AddCommMonoid ;)
AddCommMonoid : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A commutative additive semigroup is a type with an associative commutative `(+)`. ;)
(; //LEAN:AddCommSemigroup ;)
AddCommSemigroup : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: An `AddGroup` is an `AddMonoid` with a unary `-` satisfying `-a + a = 0`.  There is also a binary operation `-` such that `a - b = a + -b`, with a default so that `a - b = a + -b` holds by definition.  Use `AddGroup.ofLeftAxioms` or `AddGroup.ofRightAxioms` to define an additive group structure on a type with the minimum proof obligations.  [Wikidata Q83478](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q83478) ;)
(; //LEAN:AddGroup ;)
AddGroup : (A : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: An `AddMonoid` is an `AddSemigroup` with an element `0` such that `0 + a = a + 0 = a`. ;)
(; //LEAN:AddMonoid ;)
AddMonoid : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: An additive semigroup is a type with an associative `(+)`. ;)
(; //LEAN:AddSemigroup ;)
AddSemigroup : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Typeclass for expressing that a type `M` with addition and a zero satisfies `0 + a = a` and `a + 0 = a` for all `a : M`. ;)
(; //LEAN:AddZeroClass ;)
AddZeroClass : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Commutative version of `CancelMonoid`. ;)
(; //LEAN:CancelCommMonoid ;)
CancelCommMonoid : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A commutative group is a group with commutative `(*)`.  [Wikidata Q181296](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q181296) ;)
(; //LEAN:CommGroup ;)
CommGroup : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A commutative monoid is a monoid with commutative `(*)`. ;)
(; //LEAN:CommMonoid ;)
CommMonoid : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A commutative semigroup is a type with an associative commutative `(*)`. ;)
(; //LEAN:CommSemigroup ;)
CommSemigroup : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A `DivInvMonoid` is a `Monoid` with operations `/` and `⁻¹` satisfying `div_eq_mul_inv : ∀ a b, a / b = a * b⁻¹`.  This deduplicates the name `div_eq_mul_inv`. The default for `div` is such that `a / b = a * b⁻¹` holds by definition.  Adding `div` as a field rather than defining `a / b := a * b⁻¹` allows us to avoid certain classes of unification failures, for example: Let `Foo X` be a type with a `∀ X, Div (Foo X)` instance but no `∀ X, Inv (Foo X)`, e.g. when `Foo X` is a `EuclideanDomain`. Suppose we also have an instance `∀ X [Cromulent X], GroupWithZero (Foo X)`. Then the `(/)` coming from `GroupWithZero.div` cannot be definitionally equal to the `(/)` coming from `Foo.Div`.  In the same way, adding a `zpow` field makes it possible to avoid definitional failures in diamonds. See the definition of `Monoid` and Note [forgetful inheritance] for more explanations on this. ;)
(; //LEAN:DivInvMonoid ;)
DivInvMonoid : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A `DivInvMonoid` where `1⁻¹ = 1`. ;)
(; //LEAN:DivInvOneMonoid ;)
DivInvOneMonoid : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Commutative `DivisionMonoid`.  This is the immediate common ancestor of `CommGroup` and `CommGroupWithZero`. ;)
(; //LEAN:DivisionCommMonoid ;)
DivisionCommMonoid : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A `DivisionMonoid` is a `DivInvMonoid` with involutive inversion and such that `(a * b)⁻¹ = b⁻¹ * a⁻¹` and `a * b = 1 → a⁻¹ = b`.  This is the immediate common ancestor of `Group` and `GroupWithZero`. ;)
(; //LEAN:DivisionMonoid ;)
DivisionMonoid : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: The equality relation. It has one introduction rule, `Eq.refl`. We use `a = b` as notation for `Eq a b`. A fundamental property of equality is that it is an equivalence relation. ``` variable (α : Type) (a b c d : α) variable (hab : a = b) (hcb : c = b) (hcd : c = d)  example : a = d :=   Eq.trans (Eq.trans hab (Eq.symm hcb)) hcd ``` Equality is much more than an equivalence relation, however. It has the important property that every assertion respects the equivalence, in the sense that we can substitute equal expressions without changing the truth value. That is, given `h1 : a = b` and `h2 : p a`, we can construct a proof for `p b` using substitution: `Eq.subst h1 h2`. Example: ``` example (α : Type) (a b : α) (p : α → Prop)         (h1 : a = b) (h2 : p a) : p b :=   Eq.subst h1 h2  example (α : Type) (a b : α) (p : α → Prop)     (h1 : a = b) (h2 : p a) : p b :=   h1 ▸ h2 ``` The triangle in the second presentation is a macro built on top of `Eq.subst` and `Eq.symm`, and you can enter it by typing `\t`. For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality)  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `=` in identifiers is `eq`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq ;)
Eq : (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Prop.

(; doc: If `h : α = β` is a proof of type equality, then `h.mpr : β → α` is the induced "cast" operation in the reverse direction, mapping elements of `β` to elements of `α`.  You can prove theorems about the resulting element by induction on `h`, since `rfl.mpr` is definitionally the identity function. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.mpr ;)
Eq__mpr : (h : Proof (Eqt Set X Y)) -> (b : Elem Y) -> Elem X.

(; doc: `Eq.refl a : a = a` is reflexivity, the unique constructor of the equality type. See also `rfl`, which is usually used instead. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.refl ;)
Eq__refl : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X a a).

(; doc: Equality is symmetric: if `a = b` then `b = a`.  Because this is in the `Eq` namespace, if you have a variable `h : a = b`, `h.symm` can be used as shorthand for `Eq.symm h` as a proof of `b = a`.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.symm ;)
Eq__symm : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X a b)) -> Proof (Eqt X b a).

(; doc: Equality is transitive: if `a = b` and `b = c` then `a = c`.  Because this is in the `Eq` namespace, if you have variables or expressions `h₁ : a = b` and `h₂ : b = c`, you can use `h₁.trans h₂ : a = c` as shorthand for `Eq.trans h₁ h₂`.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.trans ;)
Eq__trans : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (c : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X a b)) -> (h1 : Proof (Eqt X b c)) -> Proof (Eqt X a c).

(; doc: A function is called bijective if it is both injective and surjective. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.Bijective ;)
Function__Bijective : (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; doc: A function `f : α → β` is called injective if `f x = f y` implies `x = y`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.Injective ;)
Function__Injective : (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:Function.Injective.eq_iff ;)
Function__Injective__eq_iff : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (I : Proof (Function__Injective f)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> Proof (iff (Eqt Y (f a) (f b)) (Eqt X a b)).

(; //LEAN:Function.Injective.eq_iff' ;)
Function__Injective__eq_iff' : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (I : Proof (Function__Injective f)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (c : Elem Y) -> (h : Proof (Eqt Y (f b) c)) -> Proof (iff (Eqt Y (f a) c) (Eqt X a b)).

(; doc: A function is involutive, if `f ∘ f = id`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.Involutive ;)
Function__Involutive : (f : (Elem X -> X)) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:Function.Involutive.bijective ;)
Function__Involutive__bijective : (X : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> X)) -> (h : Proof (Function__Involutive f)) -> Proof (Function__Bijective f).

(; //LEAN:Function.Involutive.comp_self ;)
Function__Involutive__comp_self : (X : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> X)) -> (h : Proof (Function__Involutive f)) -> Proof (Eqt (Elem X -> X) (Function__comp f f) id).

(; doc: An involution commutes across an equality. Compare to `Function.Injective.eq_iff`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.Involutive.eq_iff ;)
Function__Involutive__eq_iff : (X : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> X)) -> (h : Proof (Function__Involutive f)) -> (x : Elem X) -> (y : Elem X) -> Proof (iff (Eqt X (f x) y) (Eqt X x (f y))).

(; //LEAN:Function.Involutive.injective ;)
Function__Involutive__injective : (X : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> X)) -> (h : Proof (Function__Involutive f)) -> Proof (Function__Injective f).

(; //LEAN:Function.Involutive.surjective ;)
Function__Involutive__surjective : (X : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> X)) -> (h : Proof (Function__Involutive f)) -> Proof (Function__Surjective f).

(; doc: `LeftInverse g f` means that `g` is a left inverse to `f`. That is, `g ∘ f = id`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.LeftInverse ;)
Function__LeftInverse : (g : (Elem Y -> X)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; doc: `RightInverse g f` means that `g` is a right inverse to `f`. That is, `f ∘ g = id`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.RightInverse ;)
Function__RightInverse : (g : (Elem Y -> X)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; doc: A function `f : α → β` is called surjective if every `b : β` is equal to `f a` for some `a : α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.Surjective ;)
Function__Surjective : (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; doc: Function composition, usually written with the infix operator `∘`. A new function is created from two existing functions, where one function's output is used as input to the other.  Examples:  * `Function.comp List.reverse (List.drop 2) [3, 2, 4, 1] = [1, 4]`  * `(List.reverse ∘ List.drop 2) [3, 2, 4, 1] = [1, 4]`  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `∘` in identifiers is `comp`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.comp ;)
Function__comp : (f : (Elem Y -> W)) -> (g : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (x : Elem X) -> Elem W.

(; doc: A `Group` is a `Monoid` with an operation `⁻¹` satisfying `a⁻¹ * a = 1`.  There is also a division operation `/` such that `a / b = a * b⁻¹`, with a default so that `a / b = a * b⁻¹` holds by definition.  Use `Group.ofLeftAxioms` or `Group.ofRightAxioms` to define a group structure on a type with the minimum proof obligations.  [Wikidata Q83478](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q83478) ;)
(; //LEAN:Group ;)
Group : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: `a % b` computes the remainder upon dividing `a` by `b`. The meaning of this notation is type-dependent. * For `Nat` and `Int` it satisfies `a % b + b * (a / b) = a`,   and `a % 0` is defined to be `a`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `%` in identifiers is `mod`. ;)
(; //LEAN:HMod.hMod ;)
HMod__hMod : (x1 : Elem X) -> (x2 : Elem Y) -> Elem x.

(; doc: `a • b` computes the product of `a` and `b`. The meaning of this notation is type-dependent, but it is intended to be used for left actions.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `•` in identifiers is `smul`. ;)
(; //LEAN:HSMul.hSMul ;)
HSMul__hSMul : (x1 : Elem X) -> (x2 : Elem Y) -> Elem x.

(; doc: `a +ᵥ b` computes the sum of `a` and `b`. The meaning of this notation is type-dependent.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `+ᵥ` in identifiers is `vadd`. ;)
(; //LEAN:HVAdd.hVAdd ;)
HVAdd__hVAdd : (x1 : Elem X) -> (x2 : Elem Y) -> Elem x.

(; doc: **Alias** of `not_congr`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Iff.not ;)
Iff__not : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (h : Proof (iff a b)) -> Proof (iff (not a) (not b)).

(; //LEAN:Iff.rfl ;)
Iff__rfl : (a : Prop) -> Proof (iff a a).

(; //LEAN:Iff.symm ;)
Iff__symm : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (h : Proof (iff a b)) -> Proof (iff b a).

(; //LEAN:Iff.trans ;)
Iff__trans : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (c : Prop) -> (h : Proof (iff a b)) -> (h1 : Proof (iff b c)) -> Proof (iff a c).

(; //LEAN:Int.emod_add_mul_ediv ;)
Int__emod_add_mul_ediv : (a : Elem Int) -> (b : Elem Int) -> Proof (Eq (plus (HMod__hMod a b) (times b (div a b))) a).

(; doc: The absolute value of an integer is its distance from `0`.  This function is overridden by the compiler with an efficient implementation. This definition is the logical model.  Examples:  * `(7 : Int).natAbs = 7`  * `(0 : Int).natAbs = 0`  * `(-11 : Int).natAbs = 11` ;)
(; //LEAN:Int.natAbs ;)
Int__natAbs : (m : Elem Int) -> Elem Nat.

(; //LEAN:Int.sub_add_cancel ;)
Int__sub_add_cancel : (a : Elem Int) -> (b : Elem Int) -> Proof (Eq (plus (minus a b) b) a).

(; doc: `a⁻¹` computes the inverse of `a`. The meaning of this notation is type-dependent.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `⁻¹` in identifiers is `inv`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Inv.inv ;)
Inv__inv : (x : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: Auxiliary typeclass for types with an involutive `Inv`. ;)
(; //LEAN:InvolutiveInv ;)
InvolutiveInv : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Auxiliary typeclass for types with an involutive `Neg`. ;)
(; //LEAN:InvolutiveNeg ;)
InvolutiveNeg : (A : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A mixin for left cancellative addition. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsLeftCancelAdd ;)
IsLeftCancelAdd : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A mixin for left cancellative multiplication. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsLeftCancelMul ;)
IsLeftCancelMul : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A mixin for right cancellative addition. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsRightCancelAdd ;)
IsRightCancelAdd : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A mixin for right cancellative multiplication. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsRightCancelMul ;)
IsRightCancelMul : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A `Monoid` is a `Semigroup` with an element `1` such that `1 * a = a * 1 = a`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Monoid ;)
Monoid : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Typeclass for expressing that a type `M` with multiplication and a one satisfies `1 * a = a` and `a * 1 = a` for all `a : M`. ;)
(; //LEAN:MulOneClass ;)
MulOneClass : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Iterate a function. ;)
(; //LEAN:Nat.iterate ;)
Nat__iterate : (op : (Elem X -> X)) -> (x : Elem Nat) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; //LEAN:Nat.sub_add_cancel ;)
Nat__sub_add_cancel : (n : Elem Nat) -> (m : Elem Nat) -> (h : Proof (Leq m n)) -> Proof (Eq (plus (minus n m) m) n).

(; doc: `-a` computes the negative or opposite of `a`. The meaning of this notation is type-dependent.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `-` in identifiers is `neg` (when used as a unary operator). ;)
(; //LEAN:Neg.neg ;)
Neg__neg : (x : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: A semigroup is a type with an associative `(*)`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Semigroup ;)
Semigroup : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: `Symm r` means that the binary relation `r` is symmetric, that is, `r a b → r b a`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Std.Symm ;)
Std__Symm : (r : (Elem X -> Elem X -> Prop)) -> Prop.

(; doc: `Total X r` means that the binary relation `r` on `X` is total, that is, `r a b` or `r b a`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Std.Total ;)
Std__Total : (r : (Elem X -> Elem X -> Prop)) -> Prop.

(; doc: A `SubNegMonoid` is an `AddMonoid` with unary `-` and binary `-` operations satisfying `sub_eq_add_neg : ∀ a b, a - b = a + -b`.  The default for `sub` is such that `a - b = a + -b` holds by definition.  Adding `sub` as a field rather than defining `a - b := a + -b` allows us to avoid certain classes of unification failures, for example: Let `foo X` be a type with a `∀ X, Sub (Foo X)` instance but no `∀ X, Neg (Foo X)`. Suppose we also have an instance `∀ X [Cromulent X], AddGroup (Foo X)`. Then the `(-)` coming from `AddGroup.sub` cannot be definitionally equal to the `(-)` coming from `Foo.Sub`.  In the same way, adding a `zsmul` field makes it possible to avoid definitional failures in diamonds. See the definition of `AddMonoid` and Note [forgetful inheritance] for more explanations on this. ;)
(; //LEAN:SubNegMonoid ;)
SubNegMonoid : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A `SubNegMonoid` where `-0 = 0`. ;)
(; //LEAN:SubNegZeroMonoid ;)
SubNegZeroMonoid : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Commutative `SubtractionMonoid`. ;)
(; //LEAN:SubtractionCommMonoid ;)
SubtractionCommMonoid : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A `SubtractionMonoid` is a `SubNegMonoid` with involutive negation and such that `-(a + b) = -b + -a` and `a + b = 0 → -a = b`. ;)
(; //LEAN:SubtractionMonoid ;)
SubtractionMonoid : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Compose two proofs by transitivity, generalized over the relations involved. ;)
(; //LEAN:Trans.trans ;)
Trans__trans : (x : Elem (r a b)) -> (x1 : Elem (s b c)) -> Elem (t a c).

(; doc: `True` is a proposition and has only an introduction rule, `True.intro : True`. In other words, `True` is simply true, and has a canonical proof, `True.intro` For more information: [Propositional Logic](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/propositions_and_proofs.html#propositional-logic) ;)
(; //LEAN:True ;)
True : Prop.

(; //LEAN:add_comm ;)
add_comm : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommMagma G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (plus a b) (plus b a)).

(; //LEAN:add_left_cancel_iff ;)
add_left_cancel_iff : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (IsLeftCancelAdd G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> (c : Elem G) -> Proof (iff (Eqt G (plus a b) (plus a c)) (Eqt G b c)).

(; //LEAN:add_neg_cancel_left ;)
add_neg_cancel_left : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddGroup G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (plus a (plus (Neg__neg a) b)) b).

(; //LEAN:add_neg_cancel_right ;)
add_neg_cancel_right : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddGroup G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (plus (plus a b) (Neg__neg b)) a).

(; //LEAN:add_nsmul ;)
add_nsmul : (M : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddMonoid M)) -> (a : Elem M) -> (m : Elem Nat) -> (n : Elem Nat) -> Proof (Eqt M (HSMul__hSMul (plus m n) a) (plus (HSMul__hSMul m a) (HSMul__hSMul n a))).

(; //LEAN:add_right_cancel_iff ;)
add_right_cancel_iff : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (IsRightCancelAdd G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> (c : Elem G) -> Proof (iff (Eqt G (plus b a) (plus c a)) (Eqt G b c)).

(; //LEAN:add_sub_assoc ;)
add_sub_assoc : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (SubNegMonoid G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> (c : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (minus (plus a b) c) (plus a (minus b c))).

(; //LEAN:add_sub_cancel_right ;)
add_sub_cancel_right : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddGroup G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (minus (plus a b) b) a).

(; //LEAN:add_zero ;)
add_zero : (M : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddZeroClass M)) -> (a : Elem M) -> Proof (Eqt M (plus a 0) a).

(; doc: Congruence in both function and argument. If `f₁ = f₂` and `a₁ = a₂` then `f₁ a₁ = f₂ a₂`. This only works for nondependent functions; the theorem statement is more complex in the dependent case.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:congr ;)
congr : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (x : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (x1 : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (x2 : Elem X) -> (x3 : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Eqt (Elem X -> Y) x x1)) -> (h1 : Proof (Eqt X x2 x3)) -> Proof (Eqt Y (x x2) (x1 x3)).

(; doc: Congruence in the function argument: if `a₁ = a₂` then `f a₁ = f a₂` for any (nondependent) function `f`. This is more powerful than it might look at first, because you can also use a lambda expression for `f` to prove that `<something containing a₁> = <something containing a₂>`. This function is used internally by tactics like `congr` and `simp` to apply equalities inside subterms.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:congrArg ;)
congrArg : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X x x1)) -> Proof (Eqt Y (f x) (f x1)).

(; doc: Similar to `congrFun` but `β` does not depend on `α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:congrFun' ;)
congrFun' : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (g : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt (Elem X -> Y) f g)) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt Y (f a) (g a)).

(; doc: "Dependent" if-then-else, normally written via the notation `if h : c then t(h) else e(h)`, is sugar for `dite c (fun h => t(h)) (fun h => e(h))`, and it is the same as `if c then t else e` except that `t` is allowed to depend on a proof `h : c`, and `e` can depend on `h : ¬c`. (Both branches use the same name for the hypothesis, even though it has different types in the two cases.)  We use this to be able to communicate the if-then-else condition to the branches. For example, `Array.get arr i h` expects a proof `h : i < arr.size` in order to avoid a bounds check, so you can write `if h : i < arr.size then arr.get i h else ...` to avoid the bounds check inside the if branch. (Of course in this case we have only lifted the check into an explicit `if`, but we could also use this proof multiple times or derive `i < arr.size` from some other proposition that we are checking in the `if`.)  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `if h : c then t else e` in identifiers is `dite` (use `left` for `t` and `right` for `e`). ;)
(; //LEAN:dite ;)
dite : (c : Prop) -> (t : (Proof c -> X)) -> (e : (Proof (not c) -> X)) -> Elem X.

(; doc: Dividing by an element is the same as multiplying by its inverse.  This is a duplicate of `DivInvMonoid.div_eq_mul_inv` ensuring that the types unfold better. ;)
(; //LEAN:div_eq_mul_inv ;)
div_eq_mul_inv : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (DivInvMonoid G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (div a b) (times a (Inv__inv b))).

(; //LEAN:div_mul_cancel ;)
div_mul_cancel : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Group G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (times (div a b) b) a).

(; //LEAN:div_self' ;)
div_self' : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Group G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (div a a) 1).

(; //LEAN:eq_comm ;)
eq_comm : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> Proof (iff (Eqt X a b) (Eqt X b a)).

(; //LEAN:eq_inv_of_mul_eq_one_left ;)
eq_inv_of_mul_eq_one_left : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (DivisionMonoid G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> (h : Proof (Eqt G (times a b) 1)) -> Proof (Eqt G a (Inv__inv b)).

(; //LEAN:eq_neg_of_add_eq_zero_left ;)
eq_neg_of_add_eq_zero_left : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (SubtractionMonoid G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> (h : Proof (Eqt G (plus a b) 0)) -> Proof (Eqt G a (Neg__neg b)).

(; //LEAN:eq_self ;)
eq_self : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt Prop (Eqt X a a) True).

(; doc: **Function extensionality.** If two functions return equal results for all possible arguments, then they are equal.  It is called “extensionality” because it provides a way to prove two objects equal based on the properties of the underlying mathematical functions, rather than based on the syntax used to denote them. Function extensionality is a theorem that can be [proved using quotient types](https://lean-lang.org/doc/reference/4.36.0-rc1/find/?domain=Verso.Genre.Manual.section&name=quotient-funext). ;)
(; //LEAN:funext ;)
funext : (X : Set) -> (x : (Elem X -> Set)) -> (f : ((x4 : Elem X) -> x x4)) -> (g : ((x3 : Elem X) -> x x3)) -> (h : Proof (forall X (x2 => Eqt (x x2) (f x2) (g x2)))) -> Proof (Eqt ((x1 : Elem X) -> x x1) f g).

(; doc: The identity function. `id` takes an implicit argument `α : Sort u` (a type in any universe), and an argument `a : α`, and returns `a`.  Although this may look like a useless function, one application of the identity function is to explicitly put a type on an expression. If `e` has type `T`, and `T'` is definitionally equal to `T`, then `@id T' e` typechecks, and Lean knows that this expression has type `T'` rather than `T`. This can make a difference for typeclass inference, since `T` and `T'` may have different typeclass instances on them. `show T' from e` is sugar for an `@id T' e` expression. ;)
(; //LEAN:id ;)
id : (a : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; //LEAN:iff_self ;)
iff_self : (p : Prop) -> Proof (Eqt Prop (iff p p) True).

(; //LEAN:inv_eq_of_mul_eq_one_right ;)
inv_eq_of_mul_eq_one_right : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (DivisionMonoid G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> (h : Proof (Eqt G (times a b) 1)) -> Proof (Eqt G (Inv__inv a) b).

(; //LEAN:inv_inv ;)
inv_inv : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (InvolutiveInv G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (Inv__inv (Inv__inv a)) a).

(; //LEAN:inv_mul_cancel ;)
inv_mul_cancel : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Group G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (times (Inv__inv a) a) 1).

(; //LEAN:inv_mul_cancel_left ;)
inv_mul_cancel_left : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Group G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (times (Inv__inv a) (times a b)) b).

(; //LEAN:inv_mul_cancel_right ;)
inv_mul_cancel_right : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Group G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (times (times a (Inv__inv b)) b) a).

(; //LEAN:inv_one ;)
inv_one : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (InvOneClass G)) -> Proof (Eqt G (Inv__inv 1) 1).

(; doc: `if c then t else e` is notation for `ite c t e`, "if-then-else", which decides to return `t` or `e` depending on whether `c` is true or false. The explicit argument `c : Prop` does not have any actual computational content, but there is an additional `[Decidable c]` argument synthesized by typeclass inference which actually determines how to evaluate `c` to true or false. Write `if h : c then t else e` instead for a "dependent if-then-else" `dite`, which allows `t`/`e` to use the fact that `c` is true/false.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `if c then t else e` in identifiers is `ite` (use `left` for `t` and `right` for `e`). ;)
(; //LEAN:ite ;)
ite : (c : Prop) -> (t : Elem X) -> (e : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; //LEAN:left_inv_eq_right_inv ;)
left_inv_eq_right_inv : (M : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Monoid M)) -> (a : Elem M) -> (b : Elem M) -> (c : Elem M) -> (hba : Proof (Eqt M (times b a) 1)) -> (hac : Proof (Eqt M (times a c) 1)) -> Proof (Eqt M b c).

(; //LEAN:left_neg_eq_right_neg ;)
left_neg_eq_right_neg : (M : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddMonoid M)) -> (a : Elem M) -> (b : Elem M) -> (c : Elem M) -> (hba : Proof (Eqt M (plus b a) 0)) -> (hac : Proof (Eqt M (plus a c) 0)) -> Proof (Eqt M b c).

(; //LEAN:mt ;)
mt : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (h : Proof (if a b)) -> (h1 : Proof (not b)) -> Proof (not a).

(; //LEAN:mul_comm ;)
mul_comm : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (CommMagma G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (times a b) (times b a)).

(; //LEAN:mul_div_assoc ;)
mul_div_assoc : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (DivInvMonoid G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> (c : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (div (times a b) c) (times a (div b c))).

(; //LEAN:mul_div_cancel_right ;)
mul_div_cancel_right : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Group G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (div (times a b) b) a).

(; //LEAN:mul_inv_cancel_left ;)
mul_inv_cancel_left : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Group G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (times a (times (Inv__inv a) b)) b).

(; //LEAN:mul_inv_cancel_right ;)
mul_inv_cancel_right : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Group G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (times (times a b) (Inv__inv b)) a).

(; //LEAN:mul_left_cancel_iff ;)
mul_left_cancel_iff : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (IsLeftCancelMul G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> (c : Elem G) -> Proof (iff (Eqt G (times a b) (times a c)) (Eqt G b c)).

(; //LEAN:mul_one ;)
mul_one : (M : Set) -> (_ : Proof (MulOneClass M)) -> (a : Elem M) -> Proof (Eqt M (times a 1) a).

(; //LEAN:mul_right_cancel_iff ;)
mul_right_cancel_iff : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (IsRightCancelMul G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> (c : Elem G) -> Proof (iff (Eqt G (times b a) (times c a)) (Eqt G b c)).

(; //LEAN:neg_add_cancel ;)
neg_add_cancel : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddGroup G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (plus (Neg__neg a) a) 0).

(; //LEAN:neg_add_cancel_left ;)
neg_add_cancel_left : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddGroup G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (plus (Neg__neg a) (plus a b)) b).

(; //LEAN:neg_add_cancel_right ;)
neg_add_cancel_right : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddGroup G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (plus (plus a (Neg__neg b)) b) a).

(; //LEAN:neg_eq_of_add_eq_zero_right ;)
neg_eq_of_add_eq_zero_right : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (SubtractionMonoid G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> (h : Proof (Eqt G (plus a b) 0)) -> Proof (Eqt G (Neg__neg a) b).

(; //LEAN:neg_neg ;)
neg_neg : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (InvolutiveNeg G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (Neg__neg (Neg__neg a)) a).

(; //LEAN:neg_zero ;)
neg_zero : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NegZeroClass G)) -> Proof (Eqt G (Neg__neg 0) 0).

(; //LEAN:not_congr ;)
not_congr : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (h : Proof (iff a b)) -> Proof (iff (not a) (not b)).

(; //LEAN:of_eq_true ;)
of_eq_true : (p : Prop) -> (h : Proof (Eqt Prop p True)) -> Proof p.

(; //LEAN:one_mul ;)
one_mul : (M : Set) -> (_ : Proof (MulOneClass M)) -> (a : Elem M) -> Proof (Eqt M (times 1 a) a).

(; //LEAN:pow_add ;)
pow_add : (M : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Monoid M)) -> (a : Elem M) -> (m : Elem Nat) -> (n : Elem Nat) -> Proof (Eqt M (pow a (plus m n)) (times (pow a m) (pow a n))).

(; //LEAN:sub_add_cancel ;)
sub_add_cancel : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddGroup G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (plus (minus a b) b) a).

(; doc: Subtracting an element is the same as adding by its negative. This is a duplicate of `SubNegMonoid.sub_eq_add_neg` ensuring that the types unfold better. ;)
(; //LEAN:sub_eq_add_neg ;)
sub_eq_add_neg : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (SubNegMonoid G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (minus a b) (plus a (Neg__neg b))).

(; //LEAN:sub_self ;)
sub_self : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddGroup G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> Proof (Eqt G (minus a a) 0).

(; //LEAN:zero_add ;)
zero_add : (M : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddZeroClass M)) -> (a : Elem M) -> Proof (Eqt M (plus 0 a) a).

(; //LEAN:zpow_natCast ;)
zpow_natCast : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (DivInvMonoid G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (n : Elem Nat) -> Proof (Eqt G (pow a n) (pow a n)).

